A||N||9||jhogans||Squeeking sound when starting the car||||1||
Z||000000||jhogans||10-22-2000||11:19 PM||||I have an issue.  Whenever I start my car in the morning to go to work there is a squeeking sound which lasts about 10 seconds.  Than it is gone.  It happens when I start my car in evenings when I am leaving work.  It only happens between long starts.  For example, one morning it made the sound after it quit, I started driving.  I went to the gas station and got some gas.  When I started  it again it did NOT make the squeeking sound.    Somebody give me the 411 on my problem.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-22-2000||11:37 PM||||Welcome to the world of squeeky alt. belts!!<BR>If you own a Fiero, it will happen to you!<BR>Mine do the same thing..Ive replaced belts, alts, water pumps, tightened again, you name it, it still squeeks. I use that belt dressing about one every two weeks...only thing I can do. <BR>Its probobly the most common problem with Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-22-2000||11:38 PM||||Probally a worn or loose belt, just tighten the alt adjusting bracket/bolt. Could also be the bearings going out on a belt driven device, usally water pump. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||10-22-2000||11:50 PM||||I believe you have a large mouse nesting on top of the starter.  The mouse sleeps there most of the time, but when you start the car, it wakes him up, and he squeaks loudly in protest.  After about ten seconds of this, the mouse decides you're not going to stop the motor, and shuts up.  You know how it is when someone abruptly awakens you from a nice sleep.<P>That, or it's the belt(s).<P>My7Fieros: "If you own a Fiero, it will happen to you," c'mon! Nonsense!  I've had Fieros with quiet, trouble-free belts for years!  Never a squeaky belt yet (honest)!  <P>Having said that, tomorrow morning, all four of my Fieros will develop squeaky belts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">          ||||reg||12
Z||000004||My7Fieros||10-22-2000||11:54 PM||||BZZZCH!! BZZZCH!!! &lt;--- me shooting magical rays on your Fieros Stugood.....tomorrow, you to will have squeeky belts!!!<BR>MMMWWWHAAAA!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||10-23-2000||12:12 AM||||Aaaaarrrrggggghhhhhh!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>That's what I get for gloating! <P>Ummm...anyone got some belt dressing I kin use?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ||||reg||11
Z||000006||kinboyatuwo||10-23-2000||11:29 AM||||hey i dont have the squeeky belts either<P>but the mouse is a mojo for the car :-)<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000007||Tigger||10-23-2000||11:41 AM||||I had an alternator belt squeeking on me on startup for a while, tightened it down again, eliminated the squeek.<P>It literally exploded (sounded like a gun shot or a tire blow out) a month or two later going down the highway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Get a new belt.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||4
Z||000008||jhogans||10-24-2000||06:15 PM||||Thanks for the info.  How easy is it to replace the belt?  If I do decide to change the belt, what other stuff should I do while I am doing the belt replacement?  Should I replace any other parts while I'm at it?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||10-25-2000||12:00 PM||||If your Fiero has AC, you might want to change that belt as well.  I can't remember if you need to take that one off in order to get the alternator belt off, sorry mine doesn't have working AC.<P>For the alternator belt you'll need a 13mm wrench or socket to loosen the top alternator bolt to the bracket.  Using a pry bar down in the hole of the alternator bracket, you can release the tension and remove the belt.  The AC compressor belt is done similar.  You'll want to use the pry bar to pull on a casting tab on the alternator to get the tension right, someone will probably say you should get a belt tension gauge but a good rule of thumb I figure 1/2" play or a bit less depending on the gap between the pulleys is good.  After you start it up, watch your voltmeter and if it makes big wiggles your probably too loose. ||||reg||1
